Federated learning enables machines to collaboratively enhance a shared model by exchanging parameters rather than private data, thereby safeguarding privacy. Our paper introduces a novel federated learning approach designed specifically to mitigate "long tails"—the phenomenon where parameters receive an disproportionate number of updates due to private silos or privacy constraints causing data imbalance across distributed sources. By integrating concepts from fair learning and fairness theory, our solution ensures a more equitable allocation of model parameter updates, preventing bias toward parameter-group members with concentrated tail dominance. Empirical results demonstrate that our method outperforms existing state-of-the approach by improving fairness while delivering competitive accuracy, with theoretical proofs supporting the logarithmic bound of our update sequence in terms of time.
